<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammadiyah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammadiyah is an Indonesian Islamic organisation founded in Yogyakarta in 1912 by Ahmad Dahlan. A modernist movement, it called for a return to the Qur'an and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>, criticised practices it saw as unorthodox and promoted modern education. It runs thousands of schools, universities, hospitals and clinics and is, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahdlatul-ulama">Nahdlatul Ulama</a>, the second largest Muslim mass organisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, with tens of millions of followers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammadiyah.jpg" alt="Muhammadiyah" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: K. H. Siradj Dahlan. Vector by Hibensis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammadiyah.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Muhammadiyah, officially the Muhammadiyah Society, was established on 18 November 1912 in Yogyakarta by Ahmad Dahlan, a court official of the kraton of the Yogyakarta Sultanate and an educated Muslim scholar from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. Among the motives were what he saw as the backwardness of Muslim society and the penetration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Much influenced by the Egyptian reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Muhammad Abduh</a>, Dahlan held that modernisation and the purification of religion from syncretic practices were vital, and the movement has stressed tawhid, strict <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monotheism">monotheism</a>, from its beginning.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1913 to 1918 Muhammadiyah established five Islamic schools, and in 1919 an Islamic high school, with teachers supplied by the nationalist movement <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budi-utomo">Budi Utomo</a>. At Dahlan's death in 1923 it reported 2,622 men and 724 women as members, mostly in Yogyakarta. By 1925 it had built 55 schools and two clinics in Surabaya and Yogyakarta. After Abdul Karim Amrullah introduced it to the Minangkabau, it grew rapidly, and Minangkabau businesspeople spread it across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. In 1938 it claimed 250,000 members, 834 mosques, 31 libraries and 1,774 schools.</p><p>It has generally avoided politics and never formed a political party, unlike the traditionalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahdlatul-ulama">Nahdlatul Ulama</a>. In 1965 and 1966 it declared the extermination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-indonesia">Communist Party of Indonesia</a> a holy war, a view other Islamic groups endorsed, which contributed to the Indonesian mass killings of those years. Around the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> in 1998 its chairman, Amien Rais, founded the National Mandate Party, which has no official relationship with the organisation. It had 29 million members in 2008.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Muhammadiyah promotes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijtihad">ijtihad</a>, the derivation of Islamic law directly from the Qur'an and sunnah, and rejects taqlid, blind adherence. It is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> in theology and opposes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syncretism">syncretism</a> with animism and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist elements, as well as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> tradition that makes a shaykh the formal authority over Muslims. Its schools combine religious and secular education, and some are open to non-Muslims.</p><p>Beneath its Central Board stand boards at the provincial, regency or city, sub-district and village levels, and there are boards overseas. Its autonomous organisations include Aisyiyah for women, Pemuda Muhammadiyah for youth and the scouting body Hizbul Wathan. Its magazine Suara Muhammadiyah, begun by Dahlan in 1915, is one of the oldest publications in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. It was said to have 60 million members in 2019, largely urban and middle-class, and it had 89 universities in 2024. Its headquarters were first in Yogyakarta, but by 1970 several committees had moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammadiyah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mass Islamic organisations such as Nahdlatul Ulama and <b>Muhammadiyah</b> shape civil society.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
